@@s001 Examples: monoid in Scala
@@s002 <span>Week 9 · examples</span>
@@s003 Examples: monoid in Scala
@@s004 The code of the <a href="../teoria/s09-monoid.html">theory page</a>, in a file that compiles and runs. Every example is checked with Scala 3.8.4 and the course options (<code>-Werror</code>, <code>-source:future</code>). It does not contain the solutions of the course exercises: it uses different types and operations.
@@s005 The whole file
@@s006 Run it from the terminal with <code>scala-cli run Monoid.scala</code>. The first two lines are instructions for <code>scala-cli</code>: they give the Scala version and the compilation checks.
@@s007 Monoid.scala
@@s008 Directives for <code>scala-cli</code>. <code>-Werror</code> turns every compiler warning into an error: it is the rule of the course, because the code must compile without warnings.
@@s009 The type class. <code>[A]</code> is the type the monoid combines. <code>combine</code> is ⊕ and <code>empty</code> is the neutral element: they are the two things a monoid must have.
@@s010 First instance: string concatenation. <code>new Monoid[String]:</code> creates an anonymous value that implements the trait. <code>a1 + a2</code> concatenates, and <code>""</code> is the neutral element.
@@s011 Second instance, on the same type Int but with another operation. <code>max</code> returns the larger of the two. The neutral element is <code>Int.MinValue</code>, the smallest integer: <code>max(x, Int.MinValue) = x</code> for every x.
@@s012 <code>concatenate</code> reduces the list. It starts from the neutral element and combines one element at a time from the left. <code>m.combine</code> passes the method as a function of two arguments. With an empty list the result is the neutral element.
@@s013 Identity law, checked on a single value <code>x</code>. A test on one value does not prove the law: that is why the course uses property-based testing, which tries many random values.
@@s014 Associativity law: the two groupings of <code>x</code>, <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> must give the same result. This too is checked on a single triple of values.
@@s015 <code>length</code> is a function from String to Int. It is a homomorphism from (String, concatenation, "") to (Int, +, 0): line 34 checks it on an example.
@@s016 <code>@main</code> makes the function <code>esempiMonoid</code> the entry point of the program. Every <code>println</code> prints a result, one per line.
@@s017 The output
@@s018 Running the program shows this output. Each line corresponds to a <code>println</code>, in the same order.
@@s019 Output of the run
@@s020 <code>concatenate</code> with stringMonoid concatenates "a", "b", "c" in order: <b>abc</b>.
@@s021 The maximum of 3, 9, 4 is 9.
@@s022 Empty list: one gets the neutral element of maxMonoid, <b>Int.MinValue</b> = −2147483648.
@@s023 All <code>true</code>: the laws hold on the chosen examples, and the homomorphism <code>length</code> and the isomorphism String ↔ List[Char] work on those values.
@@s024 To remember
@@s025 Sentence for the exam
@@s026 A <em>monoid</em> is a type with an associative binary operation and an identity element.
@@s027 Warning
@@s028 The test of a law on some values does not prove the law. The course proves it with ScalaCheck, generating many values: that is why the trait has <code>object laws</code> with the properties.
